I’ll map this around your current app rather than give a generic product plan: data model first, then auth, picks flow, admin results, scoring, and deployment/security. I’ll avoid code and keep it roadmap-level.

One key design note already stands out: your current result model is “winner required,” but your admin rules need “no winner” outcomes like draw, no contest, or fight cancelled. That is the first schema change to plan before touching pages.

Thought for 1m 3s
Absolutely — roadmap first. Your current project is already close: the event page fetches one event and renders the fight picker, and the client already submits selected picks to /api/picks/submit with eventId and choices. 

 


Big-picture architecture
You want four main areas:

Public/auth area

Friends can sign up.

Friends can log in.

Logged-in users can make picks.

Event picks page

Shows the active UFC event.

Lets each user pick one fighter per fight.

Once submitted, picks are locked permanently.

If the event lock time passes, no new submissions.

Admin area

Only you can access it.

You can choose/create/update the active event.

You can enter results after the event.

Each fight result can be:

fighter A won

fighter B won

draw

no contest

cancelled / fight did not happen

Leaderboard

Shows users ranked by points.

100 points per correctly picked winner.

0 points for wrong picks.

0 points for draw/no contest/cancelled fights, no matter who someone picked.

Phase 1: Fix the data model before building pages
Your current Prisma schema is mostly pointed in the right direction: you already have users, events, fights, submissions, choices, and fight results. The main issue is that your current FightResult shape assumes there is always a winnerFighterId. That will not work cleanly for draw/no contest/cancelled outcomes.

I would evolve the schema conceptually like this:

Add user roles
Your User needs a role so /admin can be protected.

Conceptually:

User
- id
- email
- username
- password/auth relation
- role: USER | ADMIN
Default everyone to USER; manually make yourself ADMIN.

Add event status
Your Event should know where it is in the lifecycle.

EventStatus
- DRAFT
- OPEN
- LOCKED
- COMPLETED
Then Event has:

status
isActive
startsAt
lockAt
This lets the app know whether picks are open.

Change result model
Instead of requiring a winner, make result status explicit.

FightResult
- fightId
- status: PENDING | WINNER | DRAW | NO_CONTEST | CANCELLED
- winnerFighterId nullable
- method optional
- round optional
Rule:

If status = WINNER, winnerFighterId is required.
If status = DRAW, NO_CONTEST, or CANCELLED, winnerFighterId is null.
That matches your scoring rule perfectly.

Keep the one-submission rule
You already want one locked submission per user per event. This should be enforced in the database, not just in the UI. Prisma supports compound unique constraints, which are the right idea for “one row per user/event” or “one choice per submission/fight.” 

Your existing model direction already reflects that idea with a unique user/event submission and unique submission/fight choices. Keep that.

Phase 2: Authentication and friend signup
Since this is a Next.js/Prisma app, I would choose one of these two paths:

Option A: Auth.js / NextAuth
This fits your current stack well. Auth.js has a Prisma adapter, and the current docs specifically cover Prisma integration. 

Best for your app:

Email/password or magic link login
Prisma-backed users
Role field for admin
Session available server-side
Auth.js deployments also require an AUTH_SECRET environment variable for cookies/tokens. 

Option B: Hosted auth like Clerk or Supabase Auth
This is faster for signup/login UI, but you will still need to sync the user into your Prisma database so picks can be tied to users.

For your app, I would probably pick Auth.js + Prisma because your database is already central to the project.

Friend-only signup
To avoid random people joining, add one of these:

Simple invite code: everyone needs the same secret code to sign up.
Per-friend invite links: cleaner, but more work.
Manual approval: users sign up, you approve them in admin.
For MVP, use one shared invite code.

Phase 3: Event picks page
Right now your page is hardcoded to load ufc-328. That is fine for development, but the production version should probably become:

/picks
or

/event/[slug]
Then the page should:

Check if the user is logged in.

Fetch the active event.

Fetch fights and fighters.

Check whether this user already submitted picks.

Render one of three states:

Not logged in:
  show login/signup prompt

Logged in, not submitted, event open:
  show pick UI

Logged in, already submitted:
  show locked picks

Event locked:
  show event locked message
Your current client picker state is a good start: it tracks selected picks by fightId, requires all fights to be picked, and posts the choices to the submit endpoint. 


The important upgrade: the server must be the final authority. The submit endpoint must validate:

User is logged in
Event exists
Event is OPEN
Current time is before lockAt
User has not already submitted
Every fight in the event has exactly one pick
Each picked fighter belongs to that fight
Then create the submission and all choices in a transaction. Prisma transactions are designed for grouped writes that must succeed or fail together. 

Phase 4: Locked forever behavior
The UI should say something like:

Once submitted, picks cannot be changed.
But the actual lock must be enforced in the database/API.

The rules:

A user can create a PickSubmission once.
A user cannot update it.
A user cannot delete it from the normal app.
A user cannot create a second one for the same event.
So even if someone opens dev tools and tries to POST again, the server rejects it.

For extra safety, keep:

PickSubmission.isLocked = true
PickSubmission.submittedAt
unique(userId, eventId)
The unique constraint is the real lock. isLocked is useful for readability and future admin tools.

Phase 5: Admin page
Your /admin should be boring, powerful, and protected.

Admin dashboard sections
/admin
  - current active event
  - event status
  - total users
  - total submitted picks
  - missing submissions
  - results completion status
Event management
You need a way to create or replace the active event.

MVP approach:

Admin manually creates event:
  name
  slug
  startsAt
  lockAt

Admin manually adds fights:
  fighter A
  fighter B
  order
Better later:

Admin imports/scrapes UFC event
Admin reviews the scraped card
Admin clicks "Publish event"
You already have a scraper that finds the first upcoming UFC event and writes event/fight data to JSON, so that can eventually become your event import workflow. 

 


Results entry
For each fight, admin sees:

Khamzat Chimaev vs Sean Strickland
Result:
  - Pending
  - Chimaev won
  - Strickland won
  - Draw
  - No contest
  - Cancelled / did not happen
Optional fields:

method
round
notes
For your current scoring, method and round do not matter. You can store them later for fun stats, but scoring only needs winner/status.

Result finalization
I would add two admin actions:

Save draft results
Finalize results
Before finalizing, leaderboard can say “unofficial” or still be hidden.

After finalizing, leaderboard is official.

Phase 6: Leaderboard scoring
Start with computed scoring, not stored scoring.

Meaning: every time /leaderboard loads, calculate points from the database.

Scoring logic:

For each submitted pick:
  Find the fight result.

  If result.status is WINNER:
    If pickedFighterId === winnerFighterId:
      +100
    Else:
      +0

  If result.status is DRAW, NO_CONTEST, CANCELLED, or PENDING:
    +0
Leaderboard display:

Rank
Username
Total points
Correct picks
Total scored fights
Submitted at
Example:

1. Marko — 900 pts — 9/12 correct
2. Luka — 800 pts — 8/12 correct
3. Tim — 700 pts — 7/12 correct
Later you can add:

Event-by-event history
All-time leaderboard
Accuracy percentage
Perfect card badge
Most underdog picks
But MVP should only care about current event.

Phase 7: Routes to build
Here is the route map I would use:

/
  Redirect to /picks or show landing page

/login
  Login form

/signup
  Signup form with invite code

/picks
  Current active event picks page

/event/[slug]
  Optional direct event page

/leaderboard
  Current event leaderboard

/account
  User profile and submitted picks

/admin
  Admin dashboard

/admin/events
  Create/select active event

/admin/events/[eventId]
  Manage fights and lock status

/admin/results/[eventId]
  Enter fight results
Your navbar already has Make Picks, Leaderboard, and Account links, so the app shell is already aligned with this direction. 


Phase 8: Deployment plan
Your app is already using Prisma config with DATABASE_URL, so deployment should be straightforward once auth and migrations are clean. 


For deployment, you need:

Hosted Postgres database
Next.js hosting
Environment variables
Prisma migrations run on deploy
Auth secret configured
Admin user seeded or manually promoted
Environment variables:

DATABASE_URL
AUTH_SECRET
AUTH_URL / NEXTAUTH_URL depending on auth setup
INVITE_CODE
I would deploy the app first with a manual event in the DB. Do the scraper automation later.

Recommended MVP order
This is the order I would build it in:

1. Data model migration
Update the schema for:

User role
Event status
Nullable/no-winner fight results
Better result status enum
2. Auth
Build:

signup
login
logout
session check
admin role
3. Protected picks
Build:

/picks requires login
submit picks endpoint
locked submission behavior
show previous locked picks
4. Admin results
Build:

/admin protected by role
result entry form
save fight results
support winner/draw/no contest/cancelled
5. Leaderboard
Build:

score calculation
ranked leaderboard
current event only
6. Polish
Add:

countdown to lock
submission confirmation
locked-picks receipt
admin event status controls
mobile cleanup
7. Automation later
After MVP works manually:

scrape next event
import fights
update fighter images
auto-create event draft
The main design decision
I would make picks simple and permanent:

Users only pick the winner.
No method.
No round.
100 points per correct winner.
No winner result = no points.
Your existing type only stores fighterId for a draft pick, which already matches this MVP perfectly. 


Then later, once the core app is working, you can add bonus scoring for method/round if you want. But for now, winner-only is the cleanest path.


